## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the container-backed `ansible-test` sessions.
- An authorized API endpoint for the
  [Molecule scenario](./extensions/molecule/README.md).

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

`runtime-check` runs by default. It installs a built archive in isolation and
retains only `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
imports, module documentation and role loading/syntax, then runs available
unit tests from a separate fixture directory against that installation.
Role deployment behavior remains covered by Molecule.

To check the exact archive retained for a release:

```sh
uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
```

Run from the collection's Git root. If Nox is installed, use `nox`
instead of `uv run noxfile.py`.

```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # fix, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s lint         # Python, plugin YAML and typing
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint   # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-integration
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
```

`helper-lint` covers Python in `extensions/molecule/`, `roles/*/files/`,
`tools/`, `scripts/` and `tests/runtime_check.py`, using [ruff.toml](ruff.toml).
It runs by default; add it to CI configurations that list sessions explicitly.

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](antsibull-nox.toml).
Run container-backed sessions sequentially.

## Boundaries<a id="boundaries"></a>

A resource is one kind of product object and the module that manages it.
A definition in `_definitions.py` describes a definition-driven resource,
and `_engine.py` runs it. A hook-based resource needs more than a definition
can express, such as nested values, so its own module utility implements the
lifecycle's hooks. An area is a group of modules that share one module
utility, one activation scope and usually one Molecule scenario, such as the
modules for routes, gateways and their activation.

Replace the [fixture](plugins/modules/fixture.py), its definition and tests
together, and point the shared contract tests at a product resource in
`tests/unit/plugins/modules/contract_subject.py`. For a hook-based resource,
copy `_fixture_nested.py`, its module and its tests, and change the hook
methods; the order of the lifecycle steps belongs to the base. The shared base
and engine tests bring their own synthetic resource and fake product, so
replacing the fixture does not touch them.

Some files are the same in every collection rendered from foundata's module
skeleton and are updated from it rather than edited here: the lifecycle base,
the definition engine and the activation helper (`_lifecycle.py`, `_engine.py`
and `_activation.py` in `plugins/module_utils/`), the unit-test runner
`tests/unit/plugins/conftest.py`, the shared tests `test_contract.py`,
`test_engine.py`, `test_activation.py` and `test_lifecycle.py` in
`tests/unit/plugins/modules/`, and the lost-reply helper
`extensions/molecule/shared/files/loss.py`. When one of them needs a change,
make it in the skeleton so that every collection receives it; a local edit
would conflict with the next update from the skeleton.

{# skeleton-variant: appliance-invariants #}
The same holds for what the appliance variant adds: the act-once activation
(`plugins/module_utils/_act_once.py` with
`tests/unit/plugins/modules/test_act_once.py`) and the recovery drill
(`recovery.yml`, `recovery_areas.yml` and `recovery_fresh.yml` in
`extensions/molecule/shared/tasks/`, `recovery_dns.py` in
`extensions/molecule/shared/files/`, and
`extensions/molecule/shared/playbooks/recovery.yml`, which runs one area's
drill on its own).

The product supplies what the drill does:
`default/tasks/side_effect/zz_recovery.yml`, each area's hooks in
`default/tasks/recovery/<area>/` (or directly in `default/tasks/recovery/` for
a scenario with one area) and the backup and restore commands in the
inventory.
Keep `zz_recovery.yml` the last side-effect file. It skips the drill when the
credentials file names no console, and it always runs the drill when one is
named. Run an area's drill on its own before that area first writes anything
that affects the management path. The management-path guard belongs to the
product; check it against the product's real listeners and ingress.

{# end skeleton-variant #}
The shared Molecule playbooks in `extensions/molecule/shared/playbooks/` come
from the skeleton as well; they run each scenario's task files. Two files
connect the shared code to this product and keep the product's values when
the skeleton changes: `contract_subject.py` points the shared contract tests
at a product resource, and `plugins/module_utils/_binding.py` gives the
lifecycle base this collection's client, error schema and compatibility check.

`foundata.utils` owns byte transport, deadlines, JSON decoding and safe error
translation. The client, definitions, copied resource utilities, guard rules,
modules, documentation fragments, fake product, scenario hooks and inventory
extractor are product-owned. The extractor writes
`tools/inventory/<series>.yml` beside itself. That inventory is development
evidence: it never ships and nothing reads it at runtime.

